---
{
  title: "with_deadline"
}
---
### NAME

    with_deadline() - Awaits a promise under a ceiling, so work that never
    settles gives up instead of parking the awaiting frame for the life of
    the driver.

### SYNOPSIS

    async varargs mixed with_deadline( mixed p, float seconds );

### DESCRIPTION

    Awaits a promise under a ceiling, so work that never settles gives up
    instead of parking the awaiting frame for the life of the driver.

    The ceiling is a race against a timer, which is the only way to bound a
    wait on a promise you do not own — most of them cannot be cancelled at
    all (a promise_create() promise, async I/O, a call_out promise), and a
    race stops your waiting rather than the work. What the work goes on to
    do after the ceiling elapses is the caller's problem to handle, and
    callers with a transfer to tear down do exactly that.

    The losing arm is released on every path, not just the winning one:
    promise_race() does not stop its losers, so work that settles quickly
    would otherwise leave the timer parked for the rest of its delay,
    holding a call_out and a suspension slot. The release is a defer()
    rather than code after the await so it also runs when this body is
    itself cancelled or the work rejects. Cancelling a timer that already
    won returns 0 and does nothing.

    A non-promise is returned as-is, so wrapping something that turns out to
    be synchronous costs nothing and arms no timer. A non-positive ceiling
    means no ceiling, which only makes sense for something certain to
    settle.

### PARAMETERS

    p        (mixed)
             The promise to await, or any plain value.

    seconds  (float, optional)
             The ceiling in seconds; non-positive waits indefinitely.

### RETURN VALUES

    (mixed) What p fulfilled with, or p itself if it was not a promise.

### ERRORS

    ASYNC_ERR_TIMEOUT if the ceiling elapses first, and whatever p rejected
    with otherwise.

### EXAMPLE

    {{di1}}mixed rows = await with_deadline(SOME_D->fetch(), 3.0);{{di0}}
